== Gameplay ==
{{hero intro
| intro = {{H|Invoker}} is one of the most difficult to learn heroes in the game, but also one of the most flexible and powerful casters. As such, his skill floor is not impossibly high, however, his skill ceiling is almost unparalleled, and thus he takes significant amounts of practice in order to learn. Because of his need for quick levels and gold, he is usually played as a mid hero. Due to Invoker's heavy level requirement in order to be effective, it is generally advised to focus on leveling two orbs and getting a value point in the third, rather than trying to level all three equally. While Invoker can be more flexible if he can cast strong spells in all categories, the lack of specialization also limits his potency in areas where his team might need him to be strong. Invoker can only truly reach his full strength at level 25, and as such he needs to be able to effectively contribute to teamfights before reaching that point.
| pros = * Extremely versatile.
* Numerous disables.
* Deadly magic combos.
* Can push quite well.
* Good solo killing ability with Quas-Exort.
| cons = * Dependent on player's skills.
* Requires learning and memorizing 10 of his abilities.
* Level dependent.
* Low base armor and agility gains make him easily killed against physical attacks.
* Slow movement speed without Wex.
* Requires significant early farm to be effective.
* Vulnerable to [[silence]].
* One of the hardest heroes to play in the game.
}}

=== {{Quas}} + {{Exort}} ===
This build improves {{H|Invoker}}'s nuking and disable potential, but leaves him lacking in reach. The main objective is to get to level 8 having 4 points in both {{A|Quas|Invoker}} and {{A|Exort|Invoker}} to gain access to two {{A|Forge Spirit|Invoker}}s, and to have several points in {{A|Quas|Invoker}} early in the game to increase {{A|Cold Snap|Invoker}} damage, {{A|Ice Wall|Invoker}} slow, and overall HP regen from active instances to sustain yourself during the laning stage. Two {{A|Forge Spirit|Invoker}}s, in combination with {{A|Cold Snap|Invoker}}, can inflict tremendous amounts of damage on a single target, which can get you kills against lone enemies without escape abilities. Invoker can also use {{A|Sun Strike|Invoker}}, {{A|Chaos Meteor|Invoker}} and {{A|Deafening Blast|Invoker}} to great effect, inflicting large amounts of Magical and Pure damage on enemies. This build is normally suited best against melee-heavy lineups, or lineups that have many heroes that need to be closer to their opponents. It can also be played as a more farming oriented build, with use of {{A|Forge Spirit|Invoker}}s and perhaps {{A|Alacrity|Invoker}} (if you have one value point in {{A|Wex|Invoker}}) to push waves and gain farm, and relocating to different areas on the map with {{I|Boots of Travel}}. This works quite well because {{A|Forge Spirit|Invoker}}s can kill one camp (or give extra damage), while {{H|Invoker}} pushes creep waves with {{A|Alacrity|Invoker}}. However, {{H|Invoker}} can also kill solo heroes and assist in teamfights. To further escalate, if he gets {{I|Aghanim's Scepter}} within 15-26 minutes of the game, his {{A|Sun Strike|Invoker}} turns into a catastrophic ability that adds more to his presence at all times. However the main focus of Quas-Exort in a game usually is to get enough farm for quite a number of items (by farming, getting solo kills and participating in teamfights), and also pushing around the map, relieving pressure from other teammates perhaps in teamfights. You don't often see many pro players going for an early win with Quas-Exort {{H|Invoker}} (if they wanted an early win, they usually would go for Quas-Wex for the added teamfight ability). A disadvantage of this build is that it requires Invoker to be very good at farming, overall map awareness, team fighting ability, and generally requires higher skill than Quas-Wex. It isn't a very good build either if the other team has a very early oriented lineup, however it doesn't mean it's a bad build early game as {{H|Invoker}} has {{A|Sun Strike|Invoker}} to assist lanes and {{A|Forge Spirit|Invoker}}s and {{A|Cold Snap|Invoker}} (situationally {{A|Chaos Meteor|Invoker}} and {{A|Deafening Blast|Invoker}}) to use in fights.

Historically the most used in the competitive scene.

=== {{Quas}} + {{Wex}} ===
This build primarily focuses on bringing disables and crowd control to the table at the cost of striking power. {{H|Invoker}}s using this build focus on casting {{A|E.M.P.|Invoker}} and {{A|Tornado|Invoker}} in teamfights in order to disrupt the enemy and destroy their ability to cast spells, while using {{A|Cold Snap|Invoker}} for single-target disable. It also specializes in ganking, allowing players to use {{A|Ghost Walk|Invoker}} to roam around the map and initiate fights with {{A|Cold Snap|Invoker}} as his initiation or {{A|Ice Wall|Invoker}} for an area-of-effect slow. With a value point in {{A|Exort|Invoker}}, Invoker can also utilize {{A|Alacrity|Invoker}} to boost the attack speed of himself or an ally, and use {{A|Deafening Blast|Invoker}} to act as an additional disable, mainly used to push enemies back. This build is usually best combined with teammates who already have damage, but are lacking crowd control such as {{H|Zeus}}, {{H|Ursa}}, {{H|Leshrac}}, and many others. A disadvantage to this build is usually the lack of farm {{H|Invoker}} usually gets compared to Quas-Exort builds. Not only that, but {{H|Invoker}} would mostly somewhat struggle in taking last hits, so it is ideal for players to use {{A|Tornado|Invoker}} just to catch last hits.

The most used recently in competitive play.

=== {{Wex}} + {{Exort}} ===
This build gives {{H|Invoker}} a combination of striking power and reach, but leaves him unable to disable opponents effectively and results in a comparatively frail caster due to lack of {{A|Quas|Invoker}}. {{H|Invoker}} should generally focus on nuking targets or providing utility via {{A|Alacrity|Invoker}}, which can greatly increase the right-clicking power of an ally or {{H|Invoker}} himself. This build gives {{H|Invoker}} the ability to deal large amounts of damage with {{A|Sun Strike|Invoker}}, {{A|Chaos Meteor|Invoker}} and {{A|Deafening Blast|Invoker}}, as well as a measure of crowd control via {{A|E.M.P.|Invoker}} and a long-reaching {{A|Tornado|Invoker}}. This ability build is most effective against heroes that are excellent at kiting their enemies, such as {{H|Drow Ranger}} and {{H|Sniper}}. However, it is also extremely potent if your allies have strong initiators such as {{H|Clockwerk}}, {{H|Jakiro}}, and {{H|Faceless Void}}. Disadvantages are that it provides next to no control at all, needing other allies to keep enemy heroes in place. Also {{H|Invoker}} isn't really designed to be a right click hero, he is more of a hero that uses powerful spells to change the tide of a fight.

Relatively rarely seen in the competitive scene.

